1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:14,288
Here is a graph with four vertices in which every vertex is joined to every other one, so it has six edges.

2
00:00:14,288 --> 00:00:19,924
Drawn as a square with both diagonals, two of its edges cross in the middle.

3
00:00:19,924 --> 00:00:27,158
Now we move one vertex into the middle, and the same six edges are drawn with no crossing at all.

4
00:00:27,516 --> 00:00:32,748
A graph is called planar when it can be drawn on the plane with no crossings.

5
00:00:32,748 --> 00:00:39,684
The first drawing had a crossing, but the second drawing has none, so this graph is planar.

6
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:44,192
The edges cut the plane into regions that we call faces.

7
00:00:44,192 --> 00:00:51,686
Our drawing has three small triangles inside and one infinite face outside, so there are four faces in all.

8
00:00:51,686 --> 00:00:59,948
Counting vertices plus faces gives four plus four, which is eight, and edges plus two gives six plus two,

9
00:00:59,948 --> 00:01:01,758
which is eight as well.

10
00:01:02,116 --> 00:01:10,512
The cube drawn flat has eight vertices, twelve edges and six faces, and eight plus six is twelve plus two.

11
00:01:10,512 --> 00:01:16,692
The ancient Greeks knew the formula for polyhedra like this cube, but they could not prove it.

12
00:01:16,692 --> 00:01:24,076
Euler saw that planar graphs are the right setting for a proof, and that is the version we prove next.

13
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:34,251
Theorem ten point three says that every connected planar graph satisfies vertices plus faces equals edges

14
00:01:34,251 --> 00:01:34,980
plus two.

15
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:41,700
We prove it by induction on the number of edges, using our four vertex graph as the example.

16
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:48,230
Right now it has six edges and four faces, and four plus four is six plus two.

17
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:56,571
The smallest case has no edges at all, just one vertex and one face, and one plus one is zero plus two.

18
00:01:56,571 --> 00:02:03,469
So the formula holds for the base case, and every bigger graph will be reduced to a smaller one.

19
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:11,160
Now take a graph with a cycle and delete one edge of it, so the two faces beside that edge merge into one.

20
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:18,712
Edges and faces each drop by one, so four plus three equals five plus two for the smaller graph.

21
00:02:18,712 --> 00:02:26,340
By induction the smaller graph obeys the formula, and putting the edge back adds one to both sides.

22
00:02:26,716 --> 00:02:32,736
We keep deleting cycle edges until no cycle is left, and what remains is a tree.

23
00:02:32,736 --> 00:02:39,134
A tree does not cut the plane, so it has one face and one edge fewer than vertices.

24
00:02:39,134 --> 00:02:46,398
Four vertices, three edges and one face fit the formula, which completes the induction.

25
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:54,300
Count the sides of every face, where a side is an edge on the boundary of that face.

26
00:02:54,300 --> 00:03:00,306
In our drawing each of the four faces has three sides, so together they have twelve.

27
00:03:00,306 --> 00:03:08,770
Every edge has a face on each of its two sides, so the total is twice the edges, and twice six is twelve.

28
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:16,158
There are no parallel edges and at least three vertices, so every face has at least three sides.

29
00:03:16,158 --> 00:03:20,474
That makes three times the faces at most twice the edges.

30
00:03:20,474 --> 00:03:26,076
Euler's formula gives the faces as edges plus two minus vertices.

31
00:03:26,076 --> 00:03:32,272
Putting that in, the edges are at most three times the vertices minus six.

32
00:03:32,650 --> 00:03:38,822
So planar graphs are sparse, and our four vertex graph meets the bound exactly,

33
00:03:38,822 --> 00:03:41,948
since three times four minus six is six.

34
00:03:41,948 --> 00:03:49,231
A connected graph on a thousand vertices can have anywhere from nine hundred ninety nine edges up to nearly

35
00:03:49,231 --> 00:03:50,252
half a million.

36
00:03:50,252 --> 00:03:56,550
If it is planar, the bound allows at most two thousand nine hundred ninety four.

37
00:03:58,216 --> 00:04:03,240
Take the complete graph on five vertices, which is called K five.

38
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:10,350
Each vertex joins the other four, so there are five times four divided by two, which is ten edges.

39
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:16,349
The bound allows at most three times five minus six, which is nine edges,

40
00:04:16,349 --> 00:04:19,964
so ten is too many and K five is not planar.

41
00:04:20,316 --> 00:04:27,632
Next is the graph of three houses and three wells, where every house is joined to every well.

42
00:04:27,632 --> 00:04:35,400
That gives six vertices and nine edges, while the bound allows three times six minus six, which is twelve.

43
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:42,044
So this graph passes the first test, and we have to think harder to show it is not planar.

44
00:04:42,416 --> 00:04:49,108
A triangle would need two houses or two wells to be joined, but houses only meet wells.

45
00:04:49,108 --> 00:04:56,174
So a flat drawing would have no triangles, and every face would have at least four sides.

46
00:04:56,550 --> 00:05:00,382
That gives four times the faces at most twice the edges,

47
00:05:00,382 --> 00:05:04,830
and then the edges are at most two times the vertices minus four.

48
00:05:04,830 --> 00:05:12,962
For six vertices that limit is eight, but we have nine edges, so K three three is not planar.

49
00:05:14,616 --> 00:05:21,542
So K five and K three three are both non-planar, and in some sense they are the only ones.

50
00:05:21,542 --> 00:05:29,106
This is made exact by Kuratowski, a Polish mathematician, and the K in K five stands for his name.

51
00:05:29,106 --> 00:05:37,990
Theorem ten point four says that a graph is non-planar exactly when it contains K five or K three three.

52
00:05:38,350 --> 00:05:44,074
Contains does not mean that a copy of K three three sits there unchanged.

53
00:05:44,074 --> 00:05:51,848
For each vertex of K three three we need one connected piece of the graph, and no two pieces share a vertex.

54
00:05:51,848 --> 00:05:59,456
Wherever K three three has an edge, some edge of the graph must join the two matching pieces.

55
00:05:59,816 --> 00:06:06,752
If we squeeze every piece into a single vertex, a copy of K three three appears.

56
00:06:06,752 --> 00:06:12,676
The notes use exactly this idea to show that the four-dimensional cube is not planar.

57
00:06:12,676 --> 00:06:19,434
One direction is obvious, since a graph that contains a non-planar graph is itself non-planar.

58
00:06:19,434 --> 00:06:24,546
The other direction is difficult, and the notes leave its proof out.

